      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Single-arm prospective clinical trial."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine the treatment effect and MRI changes of targeted disc decompression using an intradiscal catheter for focal heating of symptomatic lumbar disc protrusions resulting in radicular pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "Percutaneous methods for treatment of lumbar disc herniations are potentially attractive alternatives to open surgical discetomies. The effects of a newly designed, percutaneous electrothermal intradiscal catheter were evaluated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Twenty-two subjects with MRI confirmed, single-level contained lumbar disc protrusions, a minimum of 8 weeks of radicular pain (average 63 weeks) and failed nonsurgical treatment were invited. Nineteen patients underwent the decompression procedure. Follow-up was performed by physical exam and pain severity visual analog scale (VAS), SF-36 bodily pain (BP)/physical functioning (PF) scores for 12 months post-procedure. MRIs were obtained prior to treatment and at 3 months post-procedure."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Eighty-four percent of treated patients were available for follow-up at 12 months. The mean reduction in leg pain VAS scores was 2.6 points (40.6%, 95% CI 1.0-4.1). The mean reduction in back pain VAS scores was 2.4 points (44.4%, 95% CI 0.8-4.0). SF-36 BP scores improved 28.8 points (95% CI 17.5-40.0) and SF-36 PF scores improved 25.4 points (95% CI 13.0-37.7) on average. Effect sizes were small for VAS scores and moderate for SF-36 scores. Fifty-four percent of disc herniations were improved or resolved on MRI. No device-related or intraoperative complications were observed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Targeted disc decompression provided moderate improvement in leg pain and function in the majority of patients with chronic radicular pain."
        }
      ]},
